# modules/dast/passive/checks.rules - the §7.1 cookie-flag script-check
# registry (rules/RULE-FORMAT.md §9.5; docs/STEP5-DAST-PLAN.md DAST-06, tier 2).
#
# ONE `checks.rules` FOR THE WHOLE §7.1 TIER, WHICH IS THE FROZEN FORMAT'S CHOICE
# AND NOT THIS TICKET'S. DAST-05..DAST-11 are peers with no ordering between
# them and are being built in parallel, so a per-owning-script pack in this
# directory would have kept each ticket's records to itself; that was this
# ticket's first attempt and it is not available. rules/RULE-FORMAT.md §9's path
# table reserves the basename `checks.rules` repository-wide for the §9.5 schema
# and a record file matching no row of that table is `E070` - measured, on
# `modules/dast/passive/cookies.rules`, by `tests/lint-rules.sh` itself. So the
# tier shares this file. A parallel passive ticket appends its own records here
# and will meet a merge conflict in this file; it is an append-only conflict
# between records that do not interact, so take both sides.
#
# The type tag is `passive`, matching the phase table's
# `passive/cookies.sh:passive` floor (modules/dast/engine.sh). Reading the
# `Set-Cookie` headers of responses to plain GET requests mutates nothing, which
# is the whole of docs/DESIGN.md §7.1's admission criterion.
#
# THREE ATTRIBUTES, FOUR CHECK IDS, AND THE FOURTH IS THE POINT. `SameSite` has
# two failure states that are different facts with different fixes: the attribute
# ABSENT (no policy stated, so the effective one is the visitor's browser default
# - Chromium applies `Lax`, other engines have shipped both `Lax` and no
# restriction at all) and the attribute PRESENT with a value that gives no
# cross-site protection (`None`, or a token no browser recognises). One id could
# only state one of them, and `check_id` is a fingerprint component, so a single
# id would make those two states one finding whose meaning flips between runs.
# The remaining components (target, method, path_template, param_location=cookie,
# param_name=<cookie name>) are what make two flags missing on one cookie two
# findings rather than one.
#
# `intrusive` is deliberately NOT set on any of these: nothing here changes target
# state, so the passive tier is the whole gate and requiring --allow-intrusive on
# top would be a second, undocumented one.

id: DAST-COOKIE-NO_SECURE-01
title: Cookie set without the Secure attribute
script: passive/cookies.sh
severity: medium
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-614
owasp: A05:2021
tags: passive
tags: quick
tags: compliance
coverage-scope: target
remediation: Set the Secure attribute on every cookie the application issues, so
  a browser only ever sends it over TLS. Serve the site over HTTPS only and add
  HSTS so the first, pre-redirect request cannot carry the cookie either. For a
  session cookie set Secure, HttpOnly and an explicit SameSite together - each
  defends a different attack and none substitutes for another.
references: CWE-614
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A05_2021-Security_Misconfiguration/

id: DAST-COOKIE-NO_HTTPONLY-01
title: Cookie set without the HttpOnly attribute
script: passive/cookies.sh
severity: medium
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-1004
owasp: A05:2021
tags: passive
tags: quick
tags: compliance
coverage-scope: target
remediation: Set the HttpOnly attribute on every cookie no client-side script
  legitimately needs to read, which for a session or authentication cookie is
  always. If a script genuinely needs a value, put that value in a separate,
  non-authenticating cookie and leave the session cookie HttpOnly. HttpOnly
  limits the damage of a cross-site-scripting flaw and does not fix one.
references: CWE-1004
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A05_2021-Security_Misconfiguration/

id: DAST-COOKIE-SAMESITE_ABSENT-01
title: Cookie set with no SameSite attribute
script: passive/cookies.sh
severity: low
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-1275
owasp: A01:2021
tags: passive
tags: compliance
coverage-scope: target
remediation: State the policy explicitly rather than relying on a browser default
  that differs between engines and changes between releases - SameSite=Lax for a
  session cookie, which still survives ordinary top-level navigation to the site,
  or SameSite=Strict where no cross-site entry point needs the cookie. Only use
  SameSite=None for a cookie a third-party context genuinely requires, and pair
  it with Secure. SameSite reduces cross-site request forgery exposure and is not
  a substitute for an anti-CSRF token on a state-changing request.
references: CWE-1275
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A01_2021-Broken_Access_Control/

id: DAST-COOKIE-SAMESITE_WEAK-01
title: Cookie set with a SameSite value that gives no cross-site protection
script: passive/cookies.sh
severity: medium
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-1275
owasp: A01:2021
tags: passive
tags: compliance
coverage-scope: target
remediation: Use SameSite=Lax or SameSite=Strict unless a third-party context
  genuinely needs the cookie. If it does, SameSite=None is only honoured when
  Secure is also set - modern browsers reject a None cookie without it outright,
  so the cookie is silently dropped rather than merely unprotected. Correct an
  unrecognised value rather than leaving it, because browsers do not agree on how
  to treat one. Protect every state-changing endpoint with an anti-CSRF token
  regardless.
references: CWE-1275
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A01_2021-Broken_Access_Control/
